{"term":"come across","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:632659fe1f6b669de0","definition":"Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see come, across.","example":"He came across the street towards me.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/come_across#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ee4795fbf5f474575b","definition":"To change sides; to cross over to work for the opposition.","example":"You argued well in court but your firm doesn't pay its lawyers well, so why don't you come across to ours?","pos":"verb","synonyms":["come over"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/come_across#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:1f2f4229c3a37f0e68","definition":"To give an appearance or impression; to project a certain image; to seem or appear (to be some way).","example":"How did she come across when you met with her?","pos":"verb","synonyms":["come off","come over"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"idiomatic","register":"idiomatic","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/come_across#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:2a2b41ea31db83e222","definition":"To find, usually by accident.","example":"In the dark he came across an old box by accident.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["come upon","run across","encounter","happen across"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"idiomatic","register":"idiomatic, transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.